# 0009. Errors: one enum per module, mapped to protocol codes at the edge

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

Each module returns its own typed error; the connection turns them into protocol error codes,
so clients never see internals.

## Technical details

**Problem.** The spec asks for typed errors and that internal errors never reach remote
clients (SPEC §5, §10).

**How it works.**
- One `thiserror` enum per module: `ProtocolError` (exists, [0002](0002-wire-protocol-ndjson.adr)),
  `BrokerError` (exists, [0003](0003-queue-actor-per-queue.adr)), later `ServerError`,
  `ClientError`.
- The connection maps each to an `ErrorCode` (`ProtocolError::code()` already does) and sends
  `ServerFrame::error`, which uses the code's fixed text. Anything unexpected is `INTERNAL`;
  details go to the log only.
- `main` returns `Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>`.
- No `unwrap`/`expect` on anything a client controls; `expect` only for broken invariants
  (e.g. a poisoned lock).

**Consequences.**
- Some boilerplate per module.
- The client-facing error set is the protocol's, changed only with the protocol.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: thiserror 2.
- Alternatives:
  - One crate-wide error enum: every module depends on every error.
  - `anyhow` everywhere: loses the types needed to pick a protocol code.
